{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_202606142111571904da32288d4567","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"There are still test failures. Let me analyze them:\n\n1. \"throws when provider is omitted\" - Expected to throw but didn't. This is because when you omit provider, the base default \"pi-lhc\" is used, but the base model \"unconfigured\" triggers a placeholder check first, before the incomplete check. The placeholder check runs before the incomplete check.\n\n2. \"throws when model is omitted\" - Expected problem to be \"incomplete\" but got \"placeholder\". Same issue - placeholder check runs first.\n\n3. \"throws when prompt is omitted\" - Expected to throw but didn't. Same issue.\n\nThe problem is in my `loadAssignments` function. The order of validation matters. Currently, I check for placeholders BEFORE checking if all fields are present. But when a field is omitted and the base default has a placeholder value, the placeholder check catches it first.\n\nLet me fix this by changing the order - check for completeness first, then check for placeholders. This way, \"incomplete\" errors will be reported when fields are missing, and \"placeholder\" errors will only be reported when all fields are present but contain placeholders.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a10094db-b1fb-4175-b007-bb5af83ee62f","uuid":"73cba01e-0c96-445e-87d5-e444449a56ab"}
