{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_202606142112014eaf92661a2041a3","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I see the issue. When we have an override like:\n```typescript\n{\n  smoothed_prompt: {\n    provider: \"anthropic\",\n    // model and prompt omitted\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe merge logic uses `override.provider ?? base.provider`, which gives us `\"anthropic\"`. For model and prompt, since they're omitted, it uses `base.model` and `base.prompt`, which are `\"unconfigured\"` and the default prompt name.\n\nSo the merged assignment becomes: `{ provider: \"anthropic\", model: \"unconfigured\", prompt: DEFAULT_PROMPT_NAMES.smoothed_prompt }`.\n\nAll three fields are present, so the completeness check passes. But then `validateAssignment` checks for placeholders, and `\"unconfigured\"` is a placeholder, so it throws with `problem: \"placeholder\"`.\n\nThe tests expect `problem: \"incomplete\"` when a field is omitted. But the current implementation treats this as a placeholder problem because the base defaults contain placeholder values.\n\nThe issue is that the default assignments shipped with the package contain \"unconfigured\" as a placeholder value. So when you merge a partial override, you get a complete assignment with placeholder values.\n\nLooking at the test expectations, they seem to want:\n- If you omit a field → \"incomplete\" error\n- If you provide all fields but they contain placeholders → \"placeholder\" error\n\nBut the current behavior is:\n- If you omit a field, it falls back to base (which may have placeholders) → \"placeholder\" error (since all fields end up present)\n\nThis is actually a design question. Should the fallback to base defaults happen BEFORE or AFTER validation?\n\nLooking at the story requirements:\n- AC-5.5: \"An incomplete or unknown assignment fails loudly at initialization with an actionable error\"\n- TC-5.6: \"Incomplete/unknown assignment fails loud at init (missing kind, unknown prompt, incomplete, placeholder)\"\n \nI see two potential approaches to resolve the validation ambiguity. One option is to modify the base defaults to remove placeholder values. Another approach involves adjusting the validation sequence to prioritize incomplete field detection before placeholder validation.\n\nThe core challenge lies in how we interpret \"incomplete\" versus \"placeholder\" when default values are present. This nuanced validation requires a careful rethinking of the current implementation's logic.\n\nThe test case reveals a subtle expectation: when a user partially specifies a kind, it should be considered \"incomplete\" even if base defaults exist. However, from a practical standpoint, complete assignments with placeholders might be more actionable for users.\n\nThe current behavior seems reasonable - reporting \"placeholder\" when assignments contain placeholder values across all fields. This approach provides clearer guidance for configuration resolution.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a10094db-b1fb-4175-b007-bb5af83ee62f","uuid":"da00b1d2-b319-4593-9349-7574452a9266"}
