{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260614211208d00c5d6a37c04f3a","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I see the issue. The test expects that when a field is omitted (provider, model, or prompt), the error should be \"incomplete\", not \"placeholder\".\n\nBut the current code merges the override with the base defaults, so all fields end up being present (from either the override or the base). Then when it validates for placeholders, it finds \"unconfigured\" in the base defaults and throws \"placeholder\".\n\nThe test expectation seems to be that if you omit a field in your config, it should be considered \"incomplete\" regardless of what the base defaults provide.\n\nBut this doesn't make practical sense - the whole point of defaults is to fill in missing values. If you explicitly omit a field, you're saying \"use the default\", not \"this is incomplete\".\n\nLet me re-read the story requirements:\n\n- AC-5.5: \"An incomplete or unknown assignment fails loudly at initialization with an actionable error, never a silent skip or a placeholder default that masks the misconfiguration.\"\n\nThe key phrase is \"a placeholder default that masks the misconfiguration\". This suggests that if we use a placeholder default to fill in a missing field, that's masking a misconfiguration. So the test is correct - if a field is omitted, we should report \"incomplete\", not silently fill it with a placeholder from the base.\n\nThe fix is to not merge with base defaults if the result would contain placeholder values. Instead, we should check:\n1. If any field is missing from the override → \"incomplete\"\n2. If all fields are present but contain placeholders → \"placeholder\"\n\nBut wait, that would mean partial overrides don't work at all. You'd have to specify all three fields (provider, model, prompt) for every kind you want to override.\n\nActually, re-reading AC-5.5 more carefully: \"An incomplete or unknown assignment fails loudly at initialization with an actionable error, never a silent skip or a placeholder default that masks the misconfiguration.\"\n\nI think the intent is:\n- If you provide a config for a kind, it must be complete (all three fields)\n\n\n- If you provide incomplete fields, it's an error\n\nThe real solution is to change the shipped defaults to use non-placeholder values. But since \"unconfigured\" is technically a placeholder, we need a nuanced validation approach. The code should prioritize \"incomplete\" errors when override fields are missing, while still detecting placeholders in the provided values. This means carefully distinguishing between missing fields and fields with placeholder-like values, ensuring clear error messaging for misconfigured assignments.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a10094db-b1fb-4175-b007-bb5af83ee62f","uuid":"5a6494d8-1fce-4b4b-aae4-7ae215b7db60"}
