{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_202606142114012a57e0749f994836","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"There are many test failures now. The issue seems to be that updating the captureConfig function broke many tests because they're now using the fake ModelCall which doesn't match what some tests expect.\n\nLooking at the failures, they're in fork.test.ts where the session_start didn't resolve a thread. This might be because the tests are expecting a specific provider behavior.\n\nLet me reconsider my approach. The issue is that I updated the captureConfig to use the new inference config shape, but this might have broken tests that were relying on the old provider-based config.\n\nActually, looking more closely at the failures, it seems like the issue might be something else. Let me check what the actual error is.\n\nWait, looking at the errors more carefully:\n1. `TC-3.2: fork seeded by replay` - Error: attachCapture: session_start did not resolve a thread\n2. `TC-3.1: source read-only` - Error: attachCapture: session_start did not resolve a thread\n\nThese are errors where session_start didn't resolve a thread. This suggests that the session_start handler might be failing early, possibly due to the inference config check I added.\n\nLet me look at my code change in index.ts where I added:\n```typescript\nif (config.value.inference === undefined) {\n  lastDiagnostic = diag(\"inference_config_missing\", \"SDK config missing inference block\");\n  return;\n}\n```\n\nThis check might be causing the issue. Let me verify if the config being created in captureConfig has the inference block properly structured.\n\nActually, the more likely issue is that the prompts in my assignments don't match the registered prompts. Let me check the DEFAULT_PROMPT_NAMES.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":nu